ces" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333620" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "degenerate"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333614" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cou