t" "star"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333678" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "star"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/21b115b90b81f2f1bf3ecad3c8e200dae0ccd50c84459e047297e70e22efcb6f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56a292c8c09b952456c28d3dc57ca148b0af5efb6d3ba9dfff5d8662116186df"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "stable"] ["text" "unstable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "unstable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "degenerate"] ["text" "star"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "q-333619" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "degenerate"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba9afb75fe1614ba99a695afee9db33f34ecd22abed6b76f367b1e2fbcc0f784"} {"question" "e-21642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/example-21642.json" "file_sha256" "1e9d68648ce0c873829fa918ced86b923e68a0ef1d19bf20c233ae1eedf8812f"} {"question" "e-21642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/01d652156896744699e5533725f537e9f203031fab369accc10c912839ebad11.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/2dff913b6a0163c9dff6e28d81690472f043de69e4796dffb58b16b933165450.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c3fe70f694806cdfa2bb64064e3e97e8142c05c249def67cd22e5c21244a233b.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/3fd44b389c505756db88609e4ed7b78cdc6a2edeeb087edf3ed20034a9c909cf.png)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/fe0e0ebbdd9f7cd29ca5d2ca7633f829e682057024ba6c783fe7e10106a0bbfd.png) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/0a6ee4e5731289a20e011783360e0b17aa8e425995e13ee34950efd31cf73922.png)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/a30588ff9f9c3f7db4b7840afddf97a38df27ef7cdef48f4a3037e76836cb6a1.png)\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/4c337f7f6b80f340859844e8217525db98230660d22e760c70218f0c282d21e9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/example-21642.json" "file_sha256" "1e9d68648ce0c873829fa918ced86b923e68a0ef1d19bf20c233ae1eedf8812f"} {"question" "e-21643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/example-21643.json" "file_sha256" "b320f23d42de0ea7947d3fd63a77d6016e63507fba088ce7dd20ac12d733fe26"} {"question" "e-21643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/15e5c3f06172ad8218375d20178b96a80de122300761fa4a615aca099d13c52a.png) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/daa87299ff267ce47cf44b06b218f533de30e780b12905d0e0a1888a50893dd0.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/869e6bf67c0a5826b8d29130e8bf2107674508a103c4fb5a3d7d70ab78577090.png)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/523dccf73169d02b6bdf4297e96ec84bcc6c43213cdd722000f3a987a534b218.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/4fc6300682b6e28d47e43802aba9427e29c606fc6c4f77edad874e7c0e4e2c01.png)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/6c84cd04f3b037bee48d02d3183114e2668fbbd110bd44e9311e7ff454db8bac.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/example-21643.json" "file_sha256" "b320f23d42de0ea7947d3fd63a77d6016e63507fba088ce7dd20ac12d733fe26"} {"question" "e-21645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/example-21645.json" "file_sha256" "d4b4bb60b30bff7899c3fa4170bfc5321ebd4a0908854fb83cce234d4ed463b7"} {"question" "e-21645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix of our system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/7d16f04f098fc32370423b1b7adaa84c14b92291bde82672b65977b0498ec011.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/32f9fd5ff34d75a7b82427a5ab7233613da3bdbaefdaef37058e65b1691b6c4a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/example-21645.json" "file_sha256" "d4b4bb60b30bff7899c3fa4170bfc5321ebd4a0908854fb83cce234d4ed463b7"}] "usage" {"e-21642" [] "q-333672" [] "e-21645" [] "e-21643" [] "q-332743" []} "basis" 2392 "retractions" [[17592186167156 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 5y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v = [2, 2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $λ = 3$ of the system's matrix $A$. The vector $w = [- 1, 0]^{T}$ is such that $(A - λI)w = v$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$e^{-t}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [17592186167156 :question/answer-fields 17592186164323] [17592186167178 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t); y^{′}(t) = y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system. The equilibrium point (origin) is a\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnode."] [17592186166477 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-8x(t); y^{′}(t) =-8y(t)$\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above."] [17592186166477 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8\n$$\n\nwith the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$, so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/24987f26ca917591ec4d8c2ddbbf8735.png)"] [17592186166478 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A$. The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}$. Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above."] [17592186166478 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$, the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}te^{-2t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}) \\\\\n&= c_{1}e^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}e^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t + 1\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/40daf39b4da4ba08b07408a1a52d58e0.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhen $c_2=1$, we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{x = te^{-2t}; y = (-t + 1)e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/93a4716b66152e1696615a978d123f9f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/c14ad78173ebf05ed8f6540484bba45a.png)"] [17592186166479 :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 6x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) + 4y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system."] [17592186166479 :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/3f407fe5a2055332f131e6ddc696a3a8.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164324 17592186164325 17592186164326 17592186164327 17592186164329 17592186164330 17592186164331 17592186164332 17592186164477 17592186164478 17592186164480 17592186164481] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164323 :answer-field/id] [17592186164323 :answer-field/key] [17592186164323 :answer-field/type] [17592186164328 :answer-field/id] [17592186164328 :answer-field/key] [17592186164328 :answer-field/type] [17592186164476 :answer-field/id] [17592186164476 :answer-field/key] [17592186164476 :answer-field/type] [17592186164479 :answer-field/id] [17592186164479 :answer-field/key] [17592186164479 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
